# Shaktiworld — Cursor Rules

## TypeScript

- Use TypeScript in strict mode (`"strict": true` in tsconfig).
- No `any` unless absolutely unavoidable, and add a `// TODO: remove any` comment when used.
- Prefer explicit return types on exported functions.

## Dependencies

- Do not add new npm dependencies (client or server) without asking first.
- If a task can be done with built-in Node/browser APIs or existing packages, do that.

## Authentication & Authorisation

- Every protected route (API or page) must check authentication server-side via Clerk middleware (`clerkMiddleware` + `getAuth`).
- Never rely solely on client-side guards (`<Show when="signed-in">`, `useAuth`) for access control — those are UX conveniences, not security.
- If a request fails auth, return 401 with a generic message. Never leak why auth failed beyond "not authenticated" or "session expired".

## Privacy & PII

- Never log personally identifiable information (email, name, phone, address, IP) to stdout, files, or external services.
- Never include PII in error responses, analytics payloads, or client-side state that could be inspected in devtools beyond what Clerk already exposes.
- Redact PII before passing data to any third-party API.

## User-Facing Copy

- All user-facing text must avoid promising outcomes.
  - ❌ "We will find you a job"
  - ❌ "We will keep you safe"
  - ❌ "We guarantee results"
  - ✅ "Explore opportunities shared by our community"
  - ✅ "Connect with resources and support networks"
  - ✅ "Access information curated by Shaktiworld members"
- The platform connects, informs, and supports. It does not deliver results on behalf of members.
- Refer to PROJECT_SPEC.md § Non-Goals before writing any copy for Services, Listening, Jobs, or Education sections.

## General

- Keep components small and single-purpose.
- Co-locate tests next to source files when tests are written.
- Prefer named exports over default exports for non-page components.
- CSS changes should use existing custom properties (see `index.css :root`) before introducing new ones.
